DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn family of functions allows the use of FPU hardware in kernel
     code.  Modern FPUs are not limited to providing hardware implementation
     for floating point arithmetic; they offer advanced accelerators for cryp‐
     tography and other computational-intensive algorithms.  These facilities
     share registers with the FPU hardware.

     Typical kernel code does not need access to the FPU.  Saving a large reg‐
     ister file on each entry to the kernel would waste time.  When kernel
     code uses the FPU, the current FPU state must be saved to avoid corrupt‐
     ing the user-mode state, and vice versa.

     The management of the save and restore is automatic.  The processor
     catches accesses to the FPU registers when the non-current context tries
     to access them.  Explicit calls are required for the allocation of the
     save area and the notification of the start and end of the code using the
     FPU.

     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__aalllloocc__ccttxx() function allocates the memory used by ffppuu__kkeerrnn
     to track the use of the FPU hardware state and the related software
     state.  The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__aalllloocc__ccttxx() function requires the _f_l_a_g_s argument,
     which currently accepts the following flags:

           FPU_KERN_NOWAIT  Do not wait for the available memory if the re‐
                            quest could not be satisfied without sleep.

           0                No special handling is required.

     The function returns the allocated context area, or _N_U_L_L if the alloca‐
     tion failed.

     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__ffrreeee__ccttxx() function frees the context previously allocated
     by ffppuu__kkeerrnn__aalllloocc__ccttxx().

     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr() function designates the start of the region of ker‐
     nel code where the use of the FPU is allowed.  Its arguments are:

           _t_d   Currently must be _c_u_r_t_h_r_e_a_d.

           _c_t_x  The context save area previously allocated by
                ffppuu__kkeerrnn__aalllloocc__ccttxx() and not currently in use by another call
                to ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr().

           _f_l_a_g_s
                This argument currently accepts the following flags:

                      FPU_KERN_NORMAL  Indicates that the caller intends to
                                       access the full FPU state.  Must be
                                       specified currently.

                      FPU_KERN_KTHR    Indicates that no saving of the current
                                       FPU state should be performed, if the
                                       thread called fpu_kern_thread(9) func‐
                                       tion.  This is intended to minimize
                                       code duplication in callers which could
                                       be used from both kernel thread and
                                       syscall contexts.  The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__lleeaavvee()
                                       function correctly handles such con‐
                                       texts.

                      FPU_KERN_NOCTX   Avoid nesting save area.  If the flag
                                       is specified, the _c_t_x must be passed as
                                       _N_U_L_L.  The flag should only be used for
                                       really short code blocks which can be
                                       executed in a critical section.  It
                                       avoids the need to allocate the FPU
                                       context by the cost of increased system
                                       latency.

     The function does not sleep or block.  It could cause an FPU trap during
     execution, and on the first FPU access after the function returns, as
     well as after each context switch.  On i386 and amd64 this will be the
     DDeevviiccee NNoott AAvvaaiillaabbllee exception (see Intel Software Developer Manual for
     the reference).

     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__lleeaavvee() function ends the region started by
     ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr().  It is erroneous to use the FPU in the kernel before
     ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr() or after ffppuu__kkeerrnn__lleeaavvee().  The function takes the _t_d
     thread argument, which currently must be _c_u_r_t_h_r_e_a_d, and the _c_t_x context
     pointer, previously passed to ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr().  After the function re‐
     turns, the context may be freed or reused by another invocation of
     ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr().  The function always returns 0.

     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn__tthhrreeaadd() function enables an optimization for threads which
     never leave to the usermode.  The current thread will reuse the usermode
     save area for the kernel FPU state instead of requiring an explicitly al‐
     located context.  There are no flags defined for the function, and no er‐
     ror states that the function returns.  Once this function has been
     called, neither ffppuu__kkeerrnn__eenntteerr() nor ffppuu__kkeerrnn__lleeaavvee() is required to be
     called and the fpu is available for use in the calling thread.

     The iiss__ffppuu__kkeerrnn__tthhrreeaadd() function returns the boolean indicating whether
     the current thread entered the mode enabled by ffppuu__kkeerrnn__tthhrreeaadd().  There
     is currently no flags defined for the function, the return value is true
     if the current thread have the permanent FPU save area, and false other‐
     wise.

NNOOTTEESS
     The ffppuu__kkeerrnn is currently implemented only for the i386, amd64, arm64,
     and powerpc architectures.

     There is no way to handle floating point exceptions raised from kernel
     mode.

     The unused _f_l_a_g_s arguments to the ffppuu__kkeerrnn functions are to be extended
     to allow specification of the set of the FPU hardware state used by the
     code region.  This would allow optimizations of saving and restoring the
     state.

